h1. Piano Conversion Export Data Is Structurally Aggregated and Cannot Support Accurate Reporting

h2. Type: Data Quality / Architecture

h2. Priority: High

----

h2. Summary

The Piano VX conversion export endpoint ({{/rest/export/schedule/vx/conversion}}) returns data that is *structurally aggregated*: grouped by single dimensions (device, location, page, etc.) with no event IDs, no date column, no user identity, and no cross-dimension join keys. The export format makes it impossible to do user-level analysis, cross-dimension reporting, or reliable backfills.

Piano also provides an event-level API ({{/api/v3/publisher/conversion/list}}) that returns individual conversion records with unique IDs, timestamps, user identity, and payment details. This endpoint is not currently used by our pipeline.

This ticket documents the problem with evidence, compares the available approaches, and recommends a hybrid solution that uses the event-level API for conversion data while keeping the export for Exposures (the only source for paywall impression counts).

*Attached evidence (fetched April 3, 2026 from production, Cosmopolitan, date March 15, 2026):*

||File||What it is||Size||
|{{export-csvs-march-15-2026.zip}}|All 9 CSVs from the VX conversion export (aggregated)|18 KB|
|{{list-api-conversions-march-15-2026.json}}|Raw JSON from {{/conversion/list}} (38 individual events)|86 KB|
|{{list-api-conversions-march-15-2026.csv}}|Flattened CSV of the same 38 events|9 KB|

----

h2. What the Export Data Looks Like

We fetched production data for March 15, 2026 (Cosmopolitan) from the VX conversion export endpoint. It returned 9 CSV files. See {{export-csvs-march-15-2026.zip}} attached.

h3. The export produces 9 CSV files, each grouped by a single dimension

Here is what the device CSV looks like:

{noformat}
Device,Operating System,Browser,Exposures,Conversions,Conversion rate,Currency
mobile,android,chrome,12314,17,0.00138054,
mobile,ios,misc,2233,9,0.00403045,
mobile,ios,safari,5632,2,0.00035511,
other,other,misc,74,7,0.09459459,
desktop,other,chrome,53,1,0.01886792,
mobile,ios,chrome,969,1,0.00103199,
mobile,android,misc,2141,1,0.00046707,
...21 more rows with 0 conversions...
{noformat}

And the location CSV:

{noformat}
Country,Location,Exposures,Conversions,Conversion rate,Currency
N/A,N/A,3,7,2.333333,
The Netherlands,North Holland,3736,5,0.001338,
The Netherlands,South Holland,4425,4,0.000904,
Belgium,Flanders,3876,3,0.000774,
...225 more rows...
{noformat}

Each CSV has the same total: *38 conversions*. But there is:
* No date or timestamp column
* No event ID ({{term_conversion_id}})
* No user identity (uid, email)
* No payment amount
* No way to join device rows to location rows -- they share no key

h3. What the event-level API returns for the same date

Piano's {{/api/v3/publisher/conversion/list}} endpoint returns individual conversion records. We fetched the same date (March 15, 2026) and received 38 individual events -- one per conversion. See {{list-api-conversions-march-15-2026.json}} attached. Here is one event (abbreviated):

{code:json}
{
  "term_conversion_id": "TC0HTQMCP6LD",
  "type": "Registration",
  "aid": "JC6giZ1Ape",
  "create_date": 1773616906,
  "browser_id": "mmj4ybp39ycq5r8u",
  "term": {
    "term_id": "TMS2OE33RI3N",
    "name": "Maak een account aan",
    "type": "registration"
  },
  "user_access": {
    "access_id": "RFlI119N4azY",
    "granted": true,
    "start_date": 1773616905,
    "user": {
      "uid": "3c9b8af1-a1ee-433e-8272-bd1f1625e713",
      "email": "kellywarie142@gmail.com",
      "create_date": 1773616902
    }
  }
}
{code}

Every record has:
* A unique {{term_conversion_id}} (deduplatable)
* A {{create_date}} Unix timestamp (actual conversion time: March 15, 2026 23:21:46 UTC)
* User identity (uid, email)
* Term, access, subscription, and payment details all on the same record

h3. What the export cannot answer that the event-level API can

||Question||Export (current)||Event-level API ({{/conversion/list}})||
|How many total conversions?|38 (correct)|38 (correct)|
|Which users converted?|No user column in any CSV|Each record has uid and email|
|What device did user X use?|Device CSV has no user column|Enrichable via {{/conversion/data/get}} per event|
|How many Android users from Netherlands?|Device and location CSVs cannot be joined|All dimensions on same row after enrichment|
|When exactly did each conversion happen?|No date column|{{create_date}} = Unix timestamp per event|
|Can you deduplicate on reload?|No unique row identifier|{{term_conversion_id}} is unique per event|
|Can you backfill a date range?|Only day-by-day (see below)|{{create_date}} allows partitioning by day|
|Revenue per page?|No payment data in CSVs|Payment amount available per event|

----

h2. The Three Problems With Aggregated Exports

h3. 1. Cross-dimension analysis is impossible

Each export CSV is a standalone summary grouped by one dimension. There is no event ID, user ID, or foreign key shared between files. You cannot join {{conversion-device.csv}} to {{conversion-location.csv}}.

This means: revenue by page, conversions by device by country, promo code effectiveness by page -- all impossible from exports alone. The Piano dashboard can do this because it queries the underlying events.

h3. 2. Backfills must be day-by-day for exports

The export CSVs contain *no date column*. The only date information exists in the filename (derived from the {{from}}/{{to}} API parameters). This means:

* If you request {{from=2026-03-01&to=2026-03-15}}, Piano returns one set of CSVs with numbers summed over 15 days. There is no row-level indicator of which day each conversion belongs to. The result cannot be partitioned into daily slices.
* To get accurate daily data, the pipeline must call the export endpoint once per day: {{from=2026-03-15&to=2026-03-15}}, then {{from=2026-03-16&to=2026-03-16}}, etc.

*The event-level API does not have this problem.* Each record carries a {{create_date}} Unix timestamp representing the actual moment the conversion occurred. A backfill can request any date range and partition results by the event's own date. For example, the 38 records fetched for March 15 all had timestamps falling between {{2026-03-15 00:49:08 UTC}} and {{2026-03-15 23:21:46 UTC}}.

h3. 3. No deduplication is possible with exports

Export rows have no unique identifier. If the same day is loaded twice (retry, overlap, reprocessing), there is no {{term_conversion_id}} to detect duplicates. The list API returns a unique ID on every record, making deduplication trivial.

----

h2. Additional Export Issues

h3. Duplicate dimension rows within a single export

The Quest device export (6-year range) contains the same device/OS/browser appearing twice:

{noformat}
mobile,android,chrome,1761595,563,0.00031960,EUR   ← with currency
mobile,android,chrome,9085,0,0.00000000,            ← without currency
{noformat}

Piano appears to split paid vs non-paid conversions into separate rows, but provides no column to distinguish them. Loading both inflates exposure counts.

----

h2. Impact on Current Data

||What is affected||How||
|Bronze tables {{piano_reports_*}} (9 tables)|Daily data appears correct, but overlapping date ranges or multi-day fetches produce undeduplatable rows|
|Dashboards showing "conversions by X over time"|Misleading if underlying data has overlapping aggregation windows|
|Revenue by page/device/location|Impossible -- export CSVs have no payment data|
|User-level analysis|Impossible -- export CSVs have no user identity|
|Historical backfills|Must be day-by-day; multi-day ranges produce undividable aggregates|

----

h2. Approach Comparison

h3. Option A: Export-Only (current state)

Use only the VX conversion export.

||Aspect||Assessment||
|Conversion totals|Correct|
|Cross-dimension analysis|Not possible|
|User-level analysis|Not possible|
|Revenue per page/device/location|Not possible|
|Deduplication|Not possible (no event IDs)|
|Backfills|Day-by-day only|
|Exposures (paywall impressions)|Available -- only source for this|
|Complexity|Low|

*Verdict:* Suitable only if requirements are limited to single-dimension aggregate counts and Exposures. Cannot support any question that combines dimensions or identifies users.

h3. Option B: API-Only

Drop exports entirely. Use {{/conversion/list}} for event-level data, enriched with {{/conversion/data/get}} for behavioral context (device, page, location, tags).

||Aspect||Assessment||
|Conversion totals|Correct|
|Cross-dimension analysis|Full support -- all dimensions on same row|
|User-level analysis|Full support -- uid, email, access details|
|Revenue per page/device/location|Full support -- payment data + dimensions on same row|
|Deduplication|Trivial -- {{term_conversion_id}} is unique|
|Backfills|Any range -- {{create_date}} enables partitioning by day|
|Exposures (paywall impressions)|*Lost permanently* -- no API alternative|
|Conversion rate|*Lost permanently* -- derived from Exposures|
|Complexity|Higher -- pagination + N enrichment calls + rate limits|

*Verdict:* Best data quality and analytical flexibility. Choose this if Exposures and conversion rates are not needed.

h3. Option C: Hybrid (recommended)

Use {{/conversion/list}} + {{/conversion/data/get}} as the *primary source for conversion events*. Keep VX exports running *solely for Exposures*.

||Aspect||Assessment||
|Conversion totals|Correct -- from list API (event-level, deduplatable)|
|Cross-dimension analysis|Full support|
|User-level analysis|Full support|
|Revenue per page/device/location|Full support|
|Deduplication|Trivial via {{term_conversion_id}}|
|Backfills (conversions)|Any range via list API -- {{create_date}} gives actual event time|
|Backfills (exposures)|Day-by-day via export -- no date column in CSVs|
|Exposures (paywall impressions)|Preserved from VX export|
|Conversion rate|Calculable -- join list API counts with export Exposures|
|Complexity|Two parallel pipelines|

*How the hybrid works in practice:*

# *Daily conversion ingestion (list API):* Call {{/conversion/list}} with {{date_from=yesterday&date_to=today}}. For each conversion, call {{/conversion/data/get}} to get page, device, location, tags. Merge into one row per conversion with all dimensions. Load into {{bronze.piano_conversion_events}}.
# *Daily exposure ingestion (export):* Create VX export with {{from=yesterday&to=yesterday}} (always single day). Download and extract CSVs. Load into {{bronze.piano_conversion_exposures_*}}. These tables are used only for Exposures counts and conversion rate calculations.
# *Backfill conversions:* Call {{/conversion/list}} with the desired date range. Each record has {{create_date}} -- partition by day at load time. No day-by-day looping required.
# *Backfill exposures:* Must loop day-by-day. The export CSVs have no date column, so a multi-day range produces one aggregate lump that cannot be divided. Call the VX export once per day in the backfill range.

*Verdict:* Best of both worlds. Event-level data for all analytical questions, Exposures from the only source that provides them. Backfills are accurate for both paths.

----

h2. What We Gain and What We Lose

h3. Gained (not available today)

||Capability||Source||
|Individual conversion events with unique IDs|{{/conversion/list}}|
|User identity per conversion (uid, email)|{{/conversion/list}}|
|Payment amount and currency per conversion|{{/conversion/list}}|
|All dimensions on same row (device + page + location + user)|{{/conversion/list}} + {{/conversion/data/get}}|
|Geo coordinates (lat/lon/zip) per conversion|{{/conversion/data/get}}|
|Content metadata (author, section) per conversion|{{/conversion/data/get}}|
|Hour-of-day per conversion|{{create_date}} timestamp|
|Accurate backfills (any range, partition by {{create_date}})|{{/conversion/list}}|
|Deduplication via {{term_conversion_id}}|{{/conversion/list}}|
|Correct event date per record|{{create_date}} from API|

h3. Still requires exports (no API alternative)

||Field||Why||
|Exposures (paywall impression counts)|Tracked by Piano's frontend JavaScript, not stored in any API|
|Conversion rate (Conversions / Exposures)|Derived from Exposures|

h3. Lost with hybrid (acceptable)

||Field||Workaround||
|Template / Offer display names|API returns IDs only; build a lookup dimension table from VX subscription log export or manual mapping|

